[{:seon.task/name "branches-as-in-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-21: \"I want us using branches like we will in production. separate stores should be a last ditch effort where we are backing up and really trying to debug something.\" Production is one store and many branches; tests, checks and runs work the same way. Today every place makes its own database: 30 c/start! in seon.check and 6 in seon.e2e (each a fresh LMDB store seeded with 2,000 or 300 datoms: ~0.45 s each, open alone 213 ms where an mmap and a schema tx should cost a few ms; find out why), ~170 MB kept per bin/check run and unbounded across runs; and five unit-test files each carrying their own copy of a fresh in-memory db helper (merge_test, program_test, context_test, task_cost_test, program_cost_test; plus seon.db's memory cfg at db.clj:40). Make it one store: bin/check seeds one store per run and each step's root works on its own branch of it (branch! from the seeded commit); a step that proves recovery (durable, window, resume) kills and reopens that same store, as production would; bin/test makes one seeded test store and each test takes a branch of it, through one helper (the five copies go). The T5 harness and bin/root run tasks as branches of the one kept store. A separate store is a last resort, for a backup or a deep debugging session, and says why where it is made. Estimate first: a branch should cost about one commit (sub-ms) against ~450 ms per boot; disk per check run should be the seed plus each step's own writes. Measure both and put them in the commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/steps-are-branches-of-one-store seon.db-test/a-test-takes-a-branch-of-the-test-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "durable-kills-before-its-agents-flush"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by lane RUNNER (2026-10-21), 1 of 4 runs of `durable-write` then `durable-read` on one store (scratchpad sc1; the other 3 green, with and without a second root holding the store): durable-read printed `:a3 resumes :dur-clone: [[\"()\" nil]]` and DURABLE FAILED: the clone's committed note was gone. Cause (src/seon/check.clj durable-write, the clone's `(transact! [{:note/text \"the clone's note\" ...}])` then `lib/c-kill` of root): an agent's transact! is answered when committed in memory, before the sync that makes it durable (AGENTS.md: continuous group commit), and root's SIGKILL reaches the clone through the fork server's process group (kill(0,9) on EOF) before the sync that covers it. The law: a write followed by an effect outside the database waits for its flush; root's SIGKILL of the system is such an effect. Correct: phase 1's writes whose survival phase 2 asserts are made with `{:flush? true}` (or phase 1 awaits each writer's flushed fact) before the kill; a write answered unflushed and lost is then the window check's business (window-write/D), not durable's. Deletes the race."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/durable-asserts-only-flushed-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-takes-a-branch-and-deletes-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan notes/PLAN-branches.md (owner, 2026-10-22: \"The branches are supposed to be light. I'm guessing making a new store every time isn't\"; and 2026-10-21: one store, many branches, everywhere). Today `bin/check` makes a new store per run (`SEON_STORE=$OUT/store`, seeded), `seon.agent-test/in-agent` and the root tests make a store per test, single-mode `bin/check`, `e2e` and the DeepSeek check write new mains into the standing store, and `seon.test-store/test-branch!` never deletes its branch. Correct: a check run, a test and an e2e scenario each take a branch of the one store it is given and delete that branch when it ends (a failed step's branch stays until that step's next run), and never make a store; a check that kills its root proves recovery on its branch of the same store; `test-branch!` becomes a fixture that deletes its branch. Uses `datahike.api/delete-branch!`. Proof: after a check step and a test run against a store, its branch set holds nothing they made and no new store exists. Lane ONEMAIN, 2026-10-22: `seon.db/delete-main!` (a main and every branch under it, `datahike.api/delete-branch!`), `seon.check/drop-mains` (`bin/check drop MAIN...`, with the mains' agents dirs), `seon.test-store/test-branch!` takes f and deletes its branch (`seon.test-store/test-db` for read-only callers). Not done here: (1) lmdb-kill stays on an LMDB env of its own, deleted when green: it runs below Datahike, where no branch exists, and a raw put or a named sub-db in the store's env is a key in konserve's key space; (2) deleted branches are not reclaimed: `datahike.api/gc-storage` on Jolt throws `No dependency provides java.time.Instant` (probe on next-onemain/runs/store, 203 branches), so the standing store grows by what each run wrote (~320 MB for a full check) until `seon.db/the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps` lands its reclaim; (3) `seon.source-test` (a generation tree with its own runs/) and `seon.main-test` (what a fresh store holds) still make their own stores, each saying why."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-leaves-its-store-as-it-found-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-check-run-never-loses-the-seed"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-22 by lane SELECT: a `bin/check e2e loop` given a relative output path left the store's `:db` branch at 1,282 datoms instead of the seeded ~5,234, and every later `bin/check smoke` on it failed (`n-vars` 42, expected over 360); a fresh store passed. Also `seon.e2e/loop-run` with no directory builds its tree at `/tree` (root's landing run, `:clone 128 /tree`): a missing argument must refuse, not run. Correct: find what writes to `:db` (a check may only branch from it), and make loop-run's directory required by its contract."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-run-leaves-the-seed-as-it-found-it}}
