 {:seon.task/name "todays-reds-at-their-causes"
  :seon.task/plan "Lane REDS, 2026-10-22, measured on e117b9b (wave 2) in its worktree. Before: `bin/test` 335 tests, 9 failures 1 error in four tests: seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens (store identity, lane store-native's), seon.procs-test/the-flush-is-continuous-group-commit (task seon.writer/durability-is-continuous-group-commit), seon.root-test/a-build-without-whole-program-inference-reads-with-tools-reader (task jolt.ns/a-build-without-whole-program-inference-compiles-tools-reader, a Jolt fix awaiting root's go), seon.schema-test/a-registration-counts-the-stored-values-it-refuses (task my.schema/...); wave 2 had already greened procs' a-throwing-step-answers-every-pending-request and root-state-never-answers-an-empty-system, and seon.agent-test's EOF errors did not reproduce (two runs). `bin/check 100` failed 5 steps: census-3 (seon.merge/conflict-value's `:any` input, smallest [(0)]: task seon.merge/conflict-value-states-its-input), orphan (`seon.check/orphan` bounded its forever test at a literal 600000 engine ticks, written as milliseconds: the test child had ended before root died; now the run takes :seon.config.agent/test-ticks), and e2e-loop, e2e-stuck, resume: each root recorded one defect, `seon.writer/flusher-proc: nil where a java.lang.Number is required`, and the writer showed :flushes 0, so the windowed flusher threw on its notice and main was never synced by it; the continuous flusher (seon.writer task) has no window arithmetic, and the same steps show :root-errors [] and :flushes 12-31. Each fix is in the producer: the merge entry's contract, the check's bound, the writer and flusher, my.schema/registration. The Jolt red's cause and fix: task jolt.ns/a-build-without-whole-program-inference-compiles-tools-reader (Jolt branch jolt/eval-outside-build). After, on that Jolt (d7ec259b, its build-smoke passed): `bin/test` 336 tests, 1 failure (seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens, green on main through lane store-native), and `bin/check 100` CHECK OK; no orphaned holder processes after the full bin/test (bin/lanes). bin/check's census shards printed `nice(5) failed` under a niced caller: zsh's BG_NICE at the niceness ceiling; bin/check unsets it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-conflict-value-reads-a-pulled-entry seon.procs-test/the-flush-is-continuous-group-commit seon.schema-test/a-registration-counts-the-stored-values-it-refuses}}
 {:seon.task/name "mcp-reads-the-agents-records-once-committed"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-22 by lane DUP-VIEWS: bin/check 100's mcp step failed once (:both-recorded-outside false, :agent-records []) and passed twice alone. Cause: seon.check/mcp-proof reads the agent's branch (seon.root/branch-db) as soon as the MCP client exits, but the agent's record of the outside eval is applied ahead of its commit (seon.agent/transact!: committed when the agent is idle), so under load the read can come before the commit. Correct, by the harness's one way to wait (events, never a sleep, retry or deadline): tap root's events before the client runs and read the branch again at each of the agent's :commit events (seon.agent-proc emits {:type :commit :agent :branch :t}) until its records are there (seon.check/await-records). Reproduce: bin/check mcp-proof beside a full bin/test, red before, green after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/records-are-read-at-the-agents-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-report-states-src-against-the-aim"
  :seon.task/plan "Worktree session SHRINK2, 2026-10-22: `src` is 16,701 lines (wc -l over src/**/*.clj on c3265a8) against AGENTS §1's ~10k aim, and no step says so. `bin/dragons growth [BASE]` prints a branch's net delta (bin/dragons:16-21) and docs/landing.md asks every merge's commit to state it, but neither prints the total or the aim, so a merge report carries a delta with nothing to measure it against, and bin/check's verdict carries neither. Top files today: check 1,199, root 1,064, render/ui 971, reconcile 785, program 784, print/select 747, ref 608, agent 560, render/proc 487, repl 468, print/emit 436, task 380, db 374, source 373, context 361. Correct: `bin/dragons growth` also prints one line `src: N lines, aim 10000 (M over)` (N counted the way the delta is: git's tracked src files at HEAD), and bin/check's last lines repeat it beside CHECK OK/FAILED, so every merge report carries the number; the aim's one home is docs/landing.md (it is the project's measure, not a Seon runtime bound, so not a config key). Not a gate: growth stays a report, the review reads it. Once `seon.main/drivers-leave-src` lands, N counts the system without its drivers. Proof: a deftest that runs `bin/dragons growth HEAD` and reads N equal to the line count of `git ls-files src` at HEAD, and the aim equal to docs/landing.md's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/growth-states-src-against-the-aim}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-wait-is-the-event-it-means"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-22: \"fix the race condition in the tools\". The harness's doc (seon.check, top) says its waits are root's events, never a poll or a deadline, yet src/seon/check.clj kept 12 Thread/sleep windows and 5 Flow ping bounds (notes/duplicates-audit-2026-10-22.md row B7); worktree sessions saw checks red under load and green alone (the mcp step was one: task mcp-reads-the-agents-records-once-committed, whose seon.check/await-records is the pattern reused here). Cause: no event says an eval started, a test child forked, a declared proc took its messages, or a tab's view is current, so the harness slept where it meant one of those. Each site, classified: (a) a wait standing for an event becomes that event, added at the producer that knows it: the agent sends {:op :started :id} as its eval begins (seon.repl/agent-step) and its proc emits {:type :started :agent :rid} (seon.agent-proc), awaited before smoke's kill and robust's interrupt reach a running eval and before backpressure pauses the flood; the agent sends {:op :tests-started :id :pid} once its test child is forked (seon.agent/run-tests-forked) and the proc emits {:type :tests-started :agent :pid}, which orphan awaits and whose pid it prints (no pgrep); system's probe proc emits {:type :probe :tag :got} on root's events for each message it takes; the render proc tells the web proc when no request of its view is in flight after an answer ({:op :settled}), and the web proc writes an SSE comment, which idle awaits before its window; lifecycle's pause needs no window at all (Flow's control channel is FIFO: the ping after pause-proc reads :paused); drain's mid-stream sleep becomes the first half's answer. (b) a window that is the measurement (what arrives while paused, that nothing is answered while held, memory the kernel returns, idle's window) stays, its length the config key :seon.config.check/hold-ms or pages-ms, and an agent's own (Thread/sleep 2500) is the subject. (c) a Flow ping (seon.check/ping) waits :seon.config.check/ping-ms, and a proc that does not answer is recorded :blocked in the report, never read as nil. bin/lane-check's timeout 30 goes: the MCP relay answers every id (a JSON-RPC error when root gives no answer) and ends at stdin's EOF, so this merges after jolt/mcp-relay's task seon.entrance/the-door-answers-mcp-itself, not yet on main to be named in :needs (with main's seon mcp binary a root that never answers would hang the step). The windows and the ping's bound are config keys (:seon.config.check/ping-ms, hold-ms, pages-ms: AGENTS §5). bin/dragons clocks now reads seon.check and seon.e2e; their windows are named in docs/clocks.md. Proofs: the proc emits :started and :tests-started; the probe emits what it took; the render and web procs say the view is settled. Reproduce: each touched step beside a full bin/test, before and after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.check/mcp-reads-the-agents-records-once-committed"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/an-eval-says-it-started seon.procs-test/a-test-run-says-its-child-forked seon.check-test/the-probe-says-what-it-took seon.procs-test/a-tab-hears-its-view-settled}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-build-rebuilds-what-its-pin-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-22 by worktree session check-events: after a sync to land4 (Jolt 60961b84), `bin/build seon` built target/seon on the old target/jolt, and every root-booting test errored `No such var: jolt.host/runtime-fingerprint` until a full `bin/build`. Cause: bin/build's build_seon and build_hello ran `[ -x $JOLT ] || build_jolt` and `[ -f lib ] || build_lmdb|lz4|zstd`, existence checks, while build_jolt, build_chez and the library steps each already skip on their own stamp (target/NAME.stamp holding the source pin: the submodule's commit, a digest of its sources; AGENTS §5, a build's result keyed by its sources). Correct: every step calls the steps it needs unconditionally and each decides by its stamp; the existence checks go. Proof (measured, in the commit): after a pin move, `bin/build seon` rebuilds Jolt and the root-booting tests pass with no other build; with the pin unchanged, every step says up to date and `bin/build seon` takes what it took before."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "idle-opens-its-window-after-the-fork-server-caught-up"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-22 in worktree session shrink2's full bin/check 100 on 0c3bf88: idle failed with :steps-at-idle {:api [18 21] :fork-server [4 7]} in its 5 s window, green alone. Cause: main's commits reach the fork server process (seon.fork-server/server-step :tx: d/with plus program/load-changes!), which answers each with :applied; under load those answers arrive inside the window, each one step of the :fork-server proc (:ev) and one of the api (:from-fs, seon.root's :applied arm). Real work arriving late, not an idle defect: the window opens at the event that ends it. Correct: before the window idle sends {:op :stats} through the fork server (seon.root/fs-call!), a FIFO barrier: the server process takes its socket in order, so its answer follows every earlier commit's :applied through the proc and the api. Checked, not assumed: the answer's :t must have reached main's t read before the request, else :fork-server-caught-up fails (an ordering that changed fails loudly). The window still fails on any step at all. Proof: idle beside a full bin/test, red before, green after, several runs."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-root-names-its-store-and-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's ruling 2026-10-22, after task seon.source/the-restart-test-runs-its-root-on-its-own-store: a test that starts a root inherited SEON_STORE and SEON_MAIN from its caller (a bin/check run's), so its root opened a shared store whose main another run had written, and the test failed far from its cause (bin/worktree --at's exit 128). Every root-starting test gives its root an explicit store and main, through one helper, so this class cannot come back. Today: seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes now names both in its env (26b05a6); seon.root-test/root-answers names store-path by --set but leaves the main to SEON_MAIN, asks through `nc -w 30` (which on macOS waits on the open connection after the reply) and waits for the listening line by a 0.1 s poll; seon.agent-test's built-root script names its store. Correct: the one helper that launches the built binary for tests (binary-sh, from the reach-inputs branch, the natural owner; or seon.test-store) takes the store and main as arguments and always passes them, unsets SEON_STORE and SEON_MAIN for the child, reads replies from a socket (one line, ending on the reply or EOF), and waits for ROOT LISTENING on the root's output (the event), never a poll; every root-starting test calls it. Needs binary-sh on main. Proof: each root-starting test green with SEON_STORE and SEON_MAIN exported to bin/test, and a check that no test launches ./seon root but through the helper."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
